  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🛡️ Security Company Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for a guarding firm or alarm installer, from Belt & Braces Security to Bastion Guarding."
    },
    "intro": "Good security company names sound steady and dependable rather than aggressive: a surname or town with \"Security\" (<em>Hensley Security Group</em>), a strong image like <em>Ironbark</em>, or a reassuring phrase like <em>Sleep Easy Security</em>. Name your firm with this **Security Company Name Generator**: for security guards, door supervisors, event security, and CCTV and alarm installers. Pick a style and the work you do, or add your first name or town.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good security company names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Sleep Easy Security Co.</strong>: promises exactly what customers are buying.</li><li><strong>Keep Watch Security</strong>: plain, active and easy to remember.</li><li><strong>Hensley Security Group</strong>: a surname that sounds established.</li><li><strong>Sutton Security &amp; Alarms</strong>: says what you install as well as who you are.</li><li><strong>Ironbark Security Co.</strong>: a tough tree, strong without sounding aggressive.</li><li><strong>Tawny Owl Security</strong>: a night watcher that makes a good logo.</li><li><strong>Cordon Protect</strong>: a modern one-word name from event and door work.</li><li><strong>Hampton Gate Security Co.</strong>: a local name for a firm that knows the area.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Strong, not scary",
        "content": "<p>Customers are trusting you with their homes, staff and events, so the best names sound calm and capable. Surnames and towns with \"Security\" are the classic choice; tough natural images (oak, ironbark, owls, bulldogs) give you a logo; puns suit an alarm fitter better than a guarding firm. Say what you do if you can: \"Guarding\", \"CCTV\", \"Alarms\" or \"Fire &amp; Security\" helps the right customers find you.</p><p><strong>Licences:</strong> in the UK, people working as security guards, door supervisors, public-space CCTV operators, key holders and close protection officers need an SIA licence, and firms can join the SIA's Approved Contractor Scheme. Alarm and CCTV installers often gain NSI or SSAIB certification. In the US, most states license security guard companies. Never use words that suggest the police or government, such as \"Police\" or \"Official\".</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the sign",
        "content": "<p>Every name here has a Check button. Press it before you order business cards, and run through these yourself:</p><ul><li><strong>The company register:</strong> Companies House in the UK, or your state's business register in the US.</li><li><strong>Trade marks:</strong> search the UK IPO or USPTO for the same or a similar name in your field.</li><li><strong>Domains and handles:</strong> a matching .co.uk or .com, and the same handle on the platforms your clients use.</li><li><strong>Licence names:</strong> make sure the name you trade under matches the one on your licences and approvals.</li></ul><p>The <a href=\"../business-name-checker.html\">Business Name Checker</a> runs the first checks for you, and <a href=\"../guides/business-naming-best-practices.html\">Business Naming Best Practices</a> explains them in more detail. This is general guidance, not legal advice.</p><p>For the businesses you'll protect, try the <a href=\"event-planning-business-name-generator.html\">Event Planning</a> and <a href=\"property-management-name-generator.html\">Property Management</a> name generators.</p><p>For more, see all the <a href=\"../categories/biz_services-names.html\">professional and creative business name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What are good security company names?",
        "answer": "Calm, dependable names: a surname or town with Security (Hensley Security Group), a strong natural image (Ironbark Security Co.) or a reassuring phrase (Sleep Easy Security Co.)."
      },
      {
        "question": "What's a good name for a security company?",
        "answer": "Customers are trusting you with their homes, staff and events, so the name should sound steady and dependable rather than aggressive. A surname or town with \"Security\" is the classic choice (\"Hartley Security Services\"); strong words like Bastion or animals like Bulldog and Owl make a memorable logo; a light pun (\"Sure Lock Security\") suits an alarm fitter more than a guarding firm. Say what you do if you can: \"Guarding\", \"CCTV\" or \"Alarms\" helps the right customers find you."
      },
      {
        "question": "Do I need a licence to use \"Security\" in my business name?",
        "answer": "Not for the word itself, but for the work. In the UK, security guards, door supervisors, public-space CCTV operators, key holders and close protection officers need an SIA licence, and firms can join the SIA's Approved Contractor Scheme; alarm and CCTV installers often gain NSI or SSAIB certification. In the US, most states license security guard companies. Never use words that suggest the police or government, or claim an approval you don't hold."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I check a security company name is free?",
        "answer": "Press Check on any name to see whether it's free as a company, a domain and a social media handle. In the UK, search Companies House; in the US, search your state's business register and the USPTO trade mark search. Check your local area too, as two similar names nearby confuse customers."
      }
    ]
  },
